window.docContents[207].resolve({"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--What-a-partial-differential-equation-asks":{"contents":"A differential equation relates an unknown function to its derivatives, and a\npartial differential equation has several independent variables. Schematically\na PDE is a relation\n\non a domain . It may be linear or nonlinear in  and its\nderivatives, stationary or evolutionary. The notation compares values at a\npoint. Yet the questions we care about concern a whole domain or an entire\nevolution. For the rod, holding the ends at zero lets heat escape; insulating\nthem imposes a zero normal derivative and conserves total heat. The interior\nequation is the same.\n\nThe heat equation , the wave equation\n, and the incompressible Navier–Stokes equations\n\nall need this surrounding information. A domain, function spaces, boundary\nand initial data, and a meaning for the derivatives turn a differential\nidentity into a PDE problem.\n\nFor Poisson's equation, the choice of boundary data appears directly in the\nproblem statement:\n\nThe statement still leaves us work to do. Does  mean an ordinary\nrestriction of a continuous function, or a Sobolev trace? Is  continuous,\nsquare-integrable, or a distribution? The answers determine whether we seek\npointwise second derivatives or an integral identity. They also determine\nwhich estimates are available. An interior equation alone leaves the boundary\nvalue free, just as the heat residual alone did not choose the rod's ends.\n\nHadamard's well-posedness asks for existence, uniqueness, and continuous\ndependence on the data. Existence says there is a solution in our chosen class.\nUniqueness says the same data cannot produce two solutions in that class.\nContinuous dependence says that a small change in the data produces a small\nchange in the solution, measured in specified norms. This last requirement\nis what makes an approximation to the data useful.\n\nWe can see it in the rod without leaving the sine functions. Add a small,\nfiner oscillation to the initial temperature:\n\nLinearity gives the corresponding solution\n\nSubtracting the original solution  leaves just the\nperturbation. Its size in the  norm is\n\nHere the  norm measures the square root of the integrated squared\ntemperature error. Heat flow damps that error; the fine oscillation disappears\neven faster than the original profile. The formula constructs solutions for\nthese particular data. The energy argument below will explain why the\nsame stability estimate applies much more generally, and why it also gives\nuniqueness when the initial difference is zero.\n\n\n\nTrying to recover the past temperature reverses this advantage. Fix .\nThe final profiles  tend to zero in  as\n, but their initial profiles  all have norm\n. Arbitrarily small errors at the final time can therefore\nhide a substantial initial oscillation. Even on these smooth, exactly\nsolvable data, the inverse heat map is not continuous in .\nWell-posedness belongs to an equation together with its data, direction\nof evolution, solution class, and topology.\n\nFor linear second-order equations, the principal part explains much of this\nbehavior. Elliptic equations describe equilibrium. Harmonic functions obey\nmean-value identities and maximum principles, so boundary values constrain\nthe interior. Divergence-form operators\n\nare particularly suited to integration by parts: their weak formulations\nexpose bilinear forms whose coercivity can give existence and uniqueness.\nParabolic equations describe diffusion. The decay of the higher sine modes is\none manifestation of smoothing; spreading a localized pulse with a Gaussian\nis another. Hyperbolic equations describe propagation along characteristics,\nwith finite speed in the wave equation.\n\n\n\nThe three panels show a steady harmonic saddle, a spreading heat pulse, and a\ntraveling wave. The distinction matters when choosing a proof: a maximum\nprinciple controls an equilibrium through its boundary, a diffusion estimate\ncontrols smoothing, and characteristics follow information as it moves.\nNonlinearity can change the solution class itself. A smooth initial profile\nfor a conservation law may develop a shock. The distributional equation\ncontinues to make sense, but entropy inequalities are needed to select an\nadmissible solution. Hamilton–Jacobi equations instead use viscosity\nsolutions, defined through comparison with touching test functions.\n\nFluid mechanics brings these methods together. Euler describes inviscid\ntransport constrained by incompressibility; Navier–Stokes adds viscous\ndiffusion. Pressure is recovered through an elliptic equation even though\nvelocity evolves in time. Leray's global weak solutions to three-dimensional\nNavier–Stokes supply energy control in a weak solution class. Classical\nregularity and uniqueness require further arguments in a specified class.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Introduction","header":"1.1. What a partial differential equation asks","id":"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--What-a-partial-differential-equation-asks"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Cole___Hopf-Transformation--Cole___Hopf___-a-nonlinear-to-linear-transformation--Why-the-Cole___Hopf-transform-works":{"contents":"The Cole–Hopf transform (Cole, 1951; Hopf, 1950; Evans, 2010, pp. 206–207)\nsends a nonvanishing heat solution  to the solution  of\nviscous Burgers.\n\nFor the converse calculation, suppose  and  is a sufficiently\nsmooth Burgers solution on . The logarithmic derivative\ndetermines  only up to a time-dependent factor:\n\n\n\nChoosing a nonzero  satisfying this scalar equation makes  solve\nthe heat equation. Fixing  does not work in general: for \nand  it gives , whereas  is a heat solution with\nthe same Cole–Hopf quotient. We formalize the forward heat-to-Burgers\nimplication.\n\nFor that implication, suppose  is  and solves .\nDefine . Then:\n\n1. First derivative. Differentiating the quotient:\n   .2. Second derivative. Differentiating again in space:\n   .3. Time derivative. From  and :\n   .4. Assembly. The nonlinear term  combines with  and :\n   the common-denominator numerator cancels term by term, leaving\n   .\n\nThe formal proof has two layers: a jet-level polynomial cancellation that\nholds algebraically, and an analytic domain theorem that supplies the\nregularity (quotient rules, mixed-derivative commutation, nonvanishing of\n) needed to justify each step.\n\nFor a heat profile , the Cole–Hopf transform isDivision in Lean is total, so the theorems about the transform assume\nseparately that  is nonzero on the domain under consideration.\n\nThe cancellation that makes the transform work is purely algebraic and can be\nisolated first: treat\n at one point as six real numbers\n, impose  and , and check that the\nquotient-rule expressions for , , and  satisfy the\nBurgers identity.\n\nThe algebraic jet identity isolates the exact cancellation: the heat equation\nand its spatial derivative imply that the quotient-rule jets of the\nCole–Hopf transform satisfy\n\nDifferentiate  formally in  and twice in , put all\nterms over a common power of , and substitute  together\nwith . The numerator then cancels term by term,\nleaving zero. No analytic hypothesis enters at this level.\n\nTurning the jet identity into a theorem about functions requires that the\nsix jet values are the value and derivatives of , and that the quotient-rule\nexpressions give the derivatives of . That is where\n regularity, nonvanishing of , and openness of the domain enter,\nsince the quotient rule and the commutation  are local\nstatements.\n\nOn an arbitrary open spacetime domain, if , , andthen  satisfies\n\nThe analytic hypotheses justify the quotient rules and commutation of mixed\npartials. Differentiating the heat equation supplies the spatial derivative\nidentity, after which the jet-level polynomial and rational cancellation\ncloses the viscous Burgers residual.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Cole–Hopf Transformation\u0009Cole–Hopf: a nonlinear-to-linear transformation","header":"9.2.1.5.1.1. Why the Cole–Hopf transform works","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Cole___Hopf-Transformation--Cole___Hopf___-a-nonlinear-to-linear-transformation--Why-the-Cole___Hopf-transform-works"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes--Historical-notes-and-references":{"contents":"Proof-producing computation belongs to a longer line of work. Conventional\ncomputer algebra systems optimize for breadth and speed; proof assistants ask\nfor an additional object, a proposition checked by the kernel, before a result\ncan be reused as a theorem. Interval arithmetic, polynomial approximation,\nexact real arithmetic, root counting, and certified quadrature have been\nformalized in proof assistants such as HOL Light, Coq, and Isabelle.\n\nSymbolic integration.  and\n describe the theory of elementary\nintegration, including the Risch decision procedure and the Rothstein–Trager\nalgorithm. The checked search here covers bounded families of primitives and\nverifies every candidate by differentiation.\n\nTaylor models and validated numerics.  and\n provide background on Taylor models and\ntheir use in validated computation, including Taylor-model arithmetic with\nwrapping control. The Taylor-model certificates here are approximation and\ncomposition theorems for a finite polynomial with a uniform remainder.\n\nInterval arithmetic.  and\n provide general\nbackground on interval methods. We use exact rational intervals and\nproved enclosure theorems for the supported arithmetic and transcendental\noperations.\n\nModern computer arithmetic.  is a general\nreference for exact and multiple-precision arithmetic. Here the trusted\nstatements are the Lean theorems attached to the concrete arithmetic\noperations.\n\nSturm's theorem and root isolation. Starting from the real-root count of\n, the polynomial layer contains checked Sturm\nrecurrences, root counts, and root-isolation certificates, including the global\nisolator of the exact section.\n and  are useful\ncomparisons for formal real-algebraic reasoning.\n\nFormal verification of arithmetic. ,\n, ,\nand  provide background on reflection\nand proved numerical bounds in proof assistants. We follow the same\nchecked-computation pattern for the expression and\ncertificate types.\n\nLean and Mathlib.  describes the\nproof assistant, and  its mathematical library.\nThey supply the type theory, analytic definitions, and reusable proofs used\nthroughout this development.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Scope, Trust, and Notes","header":"10.10.3. Historical notes and references","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes--Historical-notes-and-references"}});